WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.990
Now, I do think it is also important to

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.950
talk about the models very quickly

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.110
because with Anthropic, there used to be

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.550
just three models in total. You had

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.190
Haiku, Sonnet, and Opus, but now they

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.830
launched a fourth tier called Fable. And

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.310
if in case you want to change your

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.630
models, that is freely accessible here

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.390
on your commands and you can just switch

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.110
the model here where you can toggle

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.590
through those four models. Now, very

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.190
simply, the way that you differentiate

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.550
between these models is that Haiku is

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.030
the least token intensive or the least

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.950
costly amongst these four, but it is

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.350
also the least powerful. And then if you

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.510
move to the right, the capabilities of

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.350
these models do increase, but the cost

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.870
also increases. Now, as a general

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.990
recommendation, I do think that if you

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.150
are only on the Pro plan, it is

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:47.950
advisable for you to try out Sonnet

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.470
first as your default because very

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.510
quickly you might run into your rate

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.350
limits, basically your token usage limit

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.630
for your 5-hour sessions or your weekly

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.310
sessions if you start to use Opus or

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.910
Fable as your default daily driver. For

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.229
me personally, I actually have Opus as

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:07.710
my default, but uh since Fable is new,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.830
I'm also just trying it out to see what

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.830
is the delta between this model versus

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.830
Opus, which is the previous tier. I

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.030
almost never use Haiku as a default

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.590
personally, but let's say you keep

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.070
hitting your rate limits, then it's

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.390
worth considering downgrading to that

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.430
model at least for very simple tasks

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.390
just to see if that will help with your

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.670
workflow. There is unfortunately no

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.790
right or wrong answer for this choice

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.350
because it really depends on the kind of

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.190
work that you do. But if you need some

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.990
guidance, then I would say to just

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.750
default to Sonnet if you are on the Pro

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.350
plan, and you can default to Opus if you

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.470
are on the Max plan. And if you need a

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.510
quick view of the cost, I have that

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.670
summarized here. And basically how to

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.150
read this is that let's say for Fable,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.310
this is the cost for a million tokens.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.470
So, it's $10 for 1 million input tokens,

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.110
and by input tokens, we just mean the

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.310
prompts that you send to the agent. So,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.830
this is commonly much cheaper versus the

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.350
output tokens. And the output tokens are

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.469
the messages, the thinking, the tool

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:09.590
calls, basically everything that your

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.630
agent does under the hood in order to

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.030
reply back to you. So, you can see here

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.630
how Fables is more expensive versus

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.710
Opus. It is literally two times more

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.390
expensive than Opus. And Opus is almost

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.750
something like 60% more expensive versus

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.949
Sonnet, which is three times more

00:02:24.959 --> 00:02:27.670
expensive versus Haiku. So, use this

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.470
table if you need a quick reference

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.070
guide. But, this just gives you a good

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.949
overview of the different incremental

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:35.270
costs across these models. I also needed

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.870
to mention that even if there is a coded

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:39.949
price here for the input tokens and the

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:41.710
output tokens, since most likely you

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.870
would be subscribed to Claude's monthly

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.510
plan, you actually don't pay those token

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.030
costs directly. Because, believe it or

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.510
not, these subscription plans are much

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.949
more subsidized versus these prices,

00:02:53.959 --> 00:02:56.230
which are the actual prices that people

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.230
pay, let's say if you're developing an

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.150
application and you are going to use

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.310
these models outside of Claude code, for

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.229
example, then these are the prices that

00:03:04.239 --> 00:03:05.949
you will pay. Also, these prices,

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:07.949
they're also the costs that you will pay

00:03:07.959 --> 00:03:09.790
if, for example, you already hit your

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.390
rate limits and you actually want to

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.470
continue using these models. Then, if

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.310
you go to your account and go to

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.430
settings here, if you go to usage, you

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.470
can also see your usage limits being

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.150
filled up. And if you scroll to the

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.750
bottom, you can see that you can turn on

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.470
usage credits to keep using Claude if

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.550
you hit a limit. But, the big caveat to

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.470
this is that the price you will pay will

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.990
be what we're calling the API prices

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.990
that we have coded here. So, just take

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.070
note because that is going to be much

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.990
more expensive versus paying through the

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.830
subscription plans that we talked about

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.360
earlier.
